---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ExtractAudioFromVideo from '@/components/tools/media/ExtractAudioFromVideo.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="extract-audio-from-video">
  <ExtractAudioFromVideo client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to extract the audio track</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in a video: MP4, MOV, MKV, WebM, AVI or MPEG.</li>
      <li>Keep the mode on <strong>Keep the original codec</strong>. FFmpeg inspects the file, finds the audio stream and copies it out unchanged.</li>
      <li>Or switch to <strong>Convert the audio</strong> if you need a specific format such as MP3 or WAV.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Extract audio</strong>. A copy is almost instant; a conversion takes about as long as the audio plays.</li>
      <li>Listen to the result and download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Demuxing versus converting</h2>
    <p>
      A video file is a container with parallel streams. Taking the audio stream out without changing the
      data is called demuxing, and this tool does it by default: FFmpeg reads the packets, writes them into
      an audio container, and never runs a decoder. The output is identical to the audio in the video, and
      the operation takes a second or two at any length.
    </p>
    <p>
      Converting is different: decode to PCM, then encode again with a new codec. If the target is MP3, AAC
      or Vorbis, that is a second lossy generation. Convert only when the destination requires that format.
    </p>

    <h2>What you get for each source codec</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Audio inside the video</th>
            <th>Usually found in</th>
            <th>Extracted as</th>
            <th>Lossless?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>AAC</td>
            <td>MP4, MOV, phone recordings</td>
            <td>.m4a</td>
            <td>Yes: copied</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Opus</td>
            <td>WebM, screen recordings, calls</td>
            <td>.ogg</td>
            <td>Yes: copied</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Vorbis</td>
            <td>Older WebM and OGV</td>
            <td>.ogg</td>
            <td>Yes: copied</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MP3</td>
            <td>AVI, older MP4</td>
            <td>.mp3</td>
            <td>Yes: copied</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>FLAC / ALAC</td>
            <td>MKV, MOV masters</td>
            <td>.flac / .m4a</td>
            <td>Yes: copied</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>AC-3 / E-AC-3</td>
            <td>MKV rips, broadcast</td>
            <td>.ac3 / .eac3</td>
            <td>Yes: copied</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      For codecs not in this list, the tool converts the audio and explains why in the result panel, so you
      do not get a file that no player can open.
    </p>

    <h2>Which tool should you use?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>This tool, when you want the audio exactly as it is in the video, in the fewest steps.</li>
      <li><a href="/video-to-mp3">Video to MP3</a>, when the destination needs MP3, or when you want to trim while you extract.</li>
      <li><a href="/audio-converter">Audio converter</a>, to change an audio file you already extracted.</li>
      <li><a href="/mute-video">Mute video</a>, to keep the picture and remove the sound.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Everything stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      FFmpeg runs as WebAssembly in the page. Recordings of meetings, lectures, therapy sessions and other
      confidential material go from your local disk into the memory of the tab and never go over a
      network. After the engine is cached once, the whole tool works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
